XI mailed you the sources either Monday or Tuesday of last week.  I can't
Xremember which day for sure.  I used regular mail, so it may take some time. 
XThe mailing required two diskettes, one for the source, the other for a compiled
Xversion of the game.  Do you have access to MPW 3.0?  You will need it to
Xcompile.  I don't think that THINK C could be used very easily in place of MPW. 
XI rely pretty heavily on the MPW environment to make the program.
X
XWhile I'm thinking about it, there are a couple of principles which, if observed
Xin all parts of the code, would make the mac port easier and less of a hack:
X
X1)  Don't modify a variable which is initialized in its declaration (e.g., "int
Xfoo_flag = TRUE").  I had to try to find all of these in order to make the game
Xrestartable.
X
X2)  Don't rely on the value of a variable which is not initialized in its
Xdeclaration being zero.  For example, get_panel() forces the panel to be redrawn
Xthe first time it is called in the course of a game because
Xpanel_[row/col]_[min/max] are all zero.  In the mac version, when the game is
Xrestarted, these variables have the values left over from the previous game.  I
Xdo with them what I do with the variables which fall under 1 above:  I keep
Xtheir "initialized" value in a resource and restore them each time a game is
Xrestarted.
X
X3) 	(This follows a fortiori from 1 above.)  Don't modify a private variable
Xwhich is initialized in its declaration.  These I had to move from the source
Xfiles in which they were declared to variables.c and externs.h in order to make
Xthem accessible for the routines which build the restart resource and copy the
Xsaved data when a new game is started.
X
XAdmittedly, there is some inconvience involved in following these rules.  It's a
Xjudgement call.
X
XThe version I have given you "hilights" the veins instead of using '%'.  I
Xdidn't discover that you had given up on hilighting until after I had ported
Xio.c to allow it.  So I figured I'd leave it in.  I discovered after I had sent
Xyou the game that this causes the map command to screw up because it doesn't
Xknow how to handle -'#'.  What do you think?  Remove the hilighting capability
Xor fix the map command to handle it?
X
XYou might want to consider the possibility of having two mac file types for save
Xfiles:  one for living characters, another for dead characters' memories.  This
Xway the two files will be distinguishable by their icons.  (Perhaps a tombstone
Xas the latter type of icon.)
X
XDo we want to get a new application signature from Apple?  I'd prefer not to,
Xbut there likely will be problems for people who try to keep the old version
Xaround on their disks.  For example, I am not certain which program will come up
Xif the user double clicks on a save file from the finder when he has both copies
Xof the game on his disk.  Maybe this can be handled by simply telling the player
Xto throw away his old version in the mac help text.
X
XI do not like the way the game "flows" at the end, particularly the standard
Xfile dialog which prompts for saving the player's memories.  It seems kind of
Xintrusive.  I do not know how things will change when the top ten is added. 
XAlso, there is a problem with the prompt in this dialog box.  In save_char() I
Xtry to say "Save your memories as:" instead of "Save the game as:" when I think
Xthe player has died or quit.  This doesn't always work right.  Sometimes it says
X"memories" when it should say "game".  This always happens when ^X is used to
Xsave the game.  I have fixed that problem since I sent you the source.  Another
Xproblem remains:  I rely upon the variable death to decide which prompt to
Xchoose.  This will not work when the player quits the character.  Death is not
Xset, yet only the memories will get saved, right?  In the version you have, I
Xhave the two prompts switched in save_char().  So, you get the "memories" prompt
Xwhen you quit and the "game" prompt when you are killed.
X
XFinally, do female dwarves really have beards?  Ugh.
X
X-Curtis (73230.224@compuserve.com)
